  "verbalization": "Butyric Acid appears to have multiple targets that ultimately lead to the B1 bradykinin receptor through a series of intermediate molecules. The evidence suggests that Butyric Acid interacts with Gamma-aminobutyric acid receptor subunit rho-3, which is targeted by R(+)-Gomisin M1, and then R(+)-Gomisin M1 further targets the B1 bradykinin receptor. Similarly, Butyric Acid also interacts with Neuraminidase, which is targeted by Annomuricin A and Sophoraflavanone A, both of which also target the B1 bradykinin receptor. These pathways highlight a potential mechanism by which Butyric Acid may influence the B1 bradykinin receptor, though the exact nature of the interaction remains unclear. The evidence supports a complex network of molecular interactions, with Butyric Acid acting as a modulator through different pathways that converge on the B1 bradykinin receptor. The integration of these pathways suggests that Butyric Acid may exert its effects through multiple mechanisms, potentially involving different receptor subunits or signaling pathways.",
